{"benchmark": "ifeval", "task_id": "1000", "sample_idx": 2, "model_name": "qwen/qwen3.5-397b-a17b", "content": "Raymond III served as the Count of Tripoli from 1152 until his death in 1187 and he played a pivotal role within the Kingdom of Jerusalem during the twelfth century. His father was Raymond II who died under suspicious circumstances while Raymond was still a minor so the young noble spent several years as a hostage in Aleppo before gaining his freedom to rule his own lands. He became known for his diplomatic skills and his ability to negotiate with Muslim leaders which set him apart from many of his contemporaries who preferred constant warfare.\n\n*Early Life and Captivity*\nThe early years of Raymond defined his cautious approach to politics because he spent roughly a decade in prison after his father was assassinated by the Hashashin order. This long period of confinement taught him patience and gave him insight into the culture and strategies of the Muslim world that surrounded the Crusader states. He eventually secured his release through a ransom agreement and returned to Tripoli to assume his duties as count while facing internal dissent and external threats from nearby powers.\n\n*Regency and Political Struggles*\nRaymond acted as regent for Baldwin IV who suffered from leprosy and could not always govern effectively so the count took charge of military campaigns and administrative decisions during this turbulent era. He opposed the faction led by Guy of Lusignan and Reynald of Châtillon because he believed their aggressive policies would provoke Saladin into launching a full-scale invasion of the kingdom. His warnings proved correct yet his advice was often ignored by the royal court leading to increased tension between the noble families and the crown.\n\n*The Battle of Hattin and Legacy*\nThe disastrous Battle of Hattin in 1187 marked the end of Raymond influence since the Crusader army was destroyed and he died shortly after the defeat possibly 